{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["And Scripture placed the matter of the wood-gatherer immediately after this passage because, by way of the plain sense, it occurred at this time, after the affair of the spies. And this is the meaning of <b>And the children of Israel were in the wilderness</b>: for when the people tarried there under the aforementioned decree, this incident occurred. Afterward (Numbers 15:38), He commanded the mitzvah of tzitzit, so that through it they would remember all the mitzvot and not forget the Sabbath or any other of the mitzvot. As for the reason why this remembrance through tzitzit should apply to all the mitzvot, Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 15:39) wrote that it is because of the numerical count of tzitzit in gematria: six hundred and eight threads plus five knots — behold, this is 613. But I do not understand this, for the word ציצת in the Torah is spelled without a yod, and its numerical value is only five hundred and ninety. Furthermore, the threads according to Beit Hillel are only three (Menahot 41a), and the knots from the Torah are only two, as they said (ibid. 39a): \"Learn from it that the upper knot is from the Torah, for if you suppose it is not from the Torah — since kilayim in tzitzit the Merciful One permitted, why do I need it? For it stands established for us that one immediate joining is not considered a connection.\" Rather, the remembrance is in the thread of tekhelet, which alludes to the all-encompassing measure that is in everything and is the purpose of everything. Therefore it says \"and you shall remember all,\" which are \"the mitzvot of HaShem.\" And this is what they said (ibid. 43b): \"Because tekhelet resembles the sea, and the sea resembles the firmament, and the firmament resembles the Throne of Glory,\" etc. And the resemblance is in the name as well as in the blue-green color, which is the culmination of appearances, for when they are distant they all appear like that color, and therefore it is called tekhelet."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "The Aramaic legal phrases from Menahot 39a are rendered hyper-literally; the word ציצת is noted as deficient spelling (missing yod) per the Masoretic text of Numbers 15:38."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:06:05.810522438Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}